29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:1:# SquashFS v1 and v2 through REA on four frozen fixtures: `squashfs_v1` and `squashfs_v2` (round 49, R49-C1 Core, 4/4 carriers)
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:5:source: S138 — https://github.com/onekey-sec/unblob/tree/26.6.4/tests/integration/filesystem/squashfs — the unblob 26.6.4 `__output__` expected member trees for `squashfs_v1` and `squashfs_v2` in both byte orders (MIT). Re-opened 2026-10-28 (receipts `S138-tag`, `S138-members-reopen`): the tag `26.6.4` peels to commit `1fcc7a0a584a70a96c31f5a276c20944d199a089`; all 22 member bytes fetched from the media base hash to the manifest sha256 with no mismatch (`members 22 mismatches 0`). Round-44 provenance: 10 contents-API listings HTTP 200, 2026-10-28T11:10:29Z–11:10:43Z; manifest `corpus/evidence/round-44/oracle/manifest.json`, sha256 `06a72dd5b660c79adae943917abf40c24ab16554ec6c2f14985958799a78f5fe` (receipt `manifest-sha`), `failures: []`
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:13:Before round 49 (2026-10-28) `PROVINCE.md` carried `SquashFS v1/v2` as still-Frontier (round-42 frontier note, line 416; receipt `province-416`: "Still Frontier: SquashFS v1/v2, `squashfs_v3_broadcom`, …"). Round 49 adds the `## Round-49 additions to Core` row `squashfs_v1` and `squashfs_v2` (SquashFS v1 and v2, gzip bodies, both byte orders) and cites this entry. Promotion outcome: moved to Core, on the four frozen carriers only, if and only if the counter is met at the landing revision (`.ufo/waves/round-49/l1/scores/c1-final.json`); the round-44 `measured, not promoted` note is superseded for v1/v2 and nothing else.
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:15:Scope of the claim, in one sentence: it is about the *handler family through REA on the four frozen carriers*, not "every SquashFS v1/v2 image on the internet". Published v1/v2 rootfs images were probed in round 44 and were not fetchable (`archive.openwrt.org/whiterussian/rc6/openwrt-brcm-2.4-squashfs.trx` returned 404 on 2026-10-28; the DD-WRT beta listing was empty), so no published carrier enters this round; the four fixtures are the whole carrier set.
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:19:unblob 26.6.4 defines the two handlers as separate classes with the same extractor (S112). `SquashFSv1Handler` (`NAME = "squashfs_v1"` :102) is matched by `73 71 73 68 [24] 00 01` (big endian) or `68 73 71 73 [24] 01 00` (little endian) (:105-121): the magic `sqsh`/`hsqs`, 24 skipped bytes, then the two bytes of the 16-bit `s_major` field, i.e. `s_major` = 1. The pattern does not constrain `s_minor`. `SquashFSv2Handler` inherits from it and only overrides the patterns (:161-178), whose `s_major` bytes are `00 02` / `02 00`, i.e. 2. Neither class overrides `EXTRACTOR`, so both use `_SquashFSBase.EXTRACTOR = SquashFSExtractor(3, 0x73717368)` (:75).
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:21:That extractor's argv decision (:37-54) branches on `endian, self.version`; only the `Endian.BIG, 4` case is special. For v1 and v2 the decision falls to `case Endian.BIG, _` → `-be` (:51-52) and `case Endian.LITTLE, _` → `-le` (:53-54), followed by the fixed tail `-no-exit-code -f -d {outdir} {inpath}` (:56-64; receipt `s112-argv`). So the command a v1/v2 chunk runs is
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:29:**Binding rule — per carrier, exact equality.** The REA `extract-firmware` receipt for carrier *X* must name *X*'s frozen handler, and the name must be equal as a whole string: `squashfs_v1` for `v1le`/`v1be`, `squashfs_v2` for `v2le`/`v2be` (frozen `handler_detail`: v1, v1, v2, v2). Prefix matching is inadmissible: `squashfs_v2` is a strict prefix of `squashfs_v2_nonstandard` (S112 :200), and `squashfs_v3` is a strict prefix of `squashfs_v3_broadcom` (:363) and `squashfs_v3_nonstandard` (:409). A prefix-keyed comparator would let a v2-nonstandard receipt satisfy a v2 carrier without any byte being wrong. Exact equality also keeps a v1 receipt from satisfying a v2 carrier, which is the point of promoting two versions rather than one.
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:33:`scripts/sb.py` (receipt `sb-decode-v2`) reads the first 32 bytes as unblob's `squashfs_super_block_t` (`char s_magic[4]`, six `uint32`, `uint16 s_major`, `uint16 s_minor`; S112 :123-137), then the v1/v2 packed tail at offset 32 (`block_size_1` u16, `block_log` u16, `flags` u8, `no_uids` u8, `no_guids` u8, `mkfs_time` u32) in the carrier's own byte order. The tail offsets are the draft layout, checked against `unsquashfs -s` (receipt `unsquashfs-s`: "Block size 32768" on the v1 pair, "Block size 65536" on the v2 pair; "Number of inodes 4/4/5/5"; "Filesystem size 177/176/201/196 bytes").
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:67:| `v1be` | `v1-be--squashfs_v1.0_be.bin` | `529e10775cc152e6c5df0c6c63316811004cc73ce99f03989c86ffd396d631e6` | 4096 | `squashfs_v1` | be | 3 | 0 | 0 | root only |
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:68:| `v1le` | `v1-le--squashfs_v1.0_le.bin` | `b45925fa779eae0293635ce0e6dc8760308f25227dd88645d677ba3ffce23e71` | 4096 | `squashfs_v1` | le | 3 | 0 | 0 | root only |
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:69:| `v2be` | `v2-be--squashfs_v2.0_be.bin` | `79c0f3886291f4abcc656acd49932446cf16afaa67e6ffb0c5258e3c2e2dfcc0` | 4096 | `squashfs_v2` | be | 4 | 0 | 0 | root only |
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:70:| `v2le` | `v2-le--squashfs_v2.0_le.bin` | `7c62dbe388b972119f5d17a12836a016737d58276184f4caa2a90dafd924f124` | 4096 | `squashfs_v2` | le | 4 | 0 | 0 | root only |
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:81:Each member digest above was also re-fetched from the S138 media base and matched (`S138-members-reopen`, 22 of 22). The two byte orders of each version hold the same member set with the same digests and sizes, even though the container bytes differ (`bytes_used` 177 vs 176 and 201 vs 196). The v1 image carries three data files plus the root directory (4 inodes); v2 carries four files plus the root (5 inodes). The S138 expected trees contain 22 members in total because they also hold the v2.1 outputs (`squashfs_v2.1_*`), which are not carriers and are not scored.
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:102:- `squashfs_v2_nonstandard` (v2ns, `sqlz`/`zlqs`): **no independent reader.** Measured 2026-10-28 on both committed v2ns fixtures: `unsquashfs` 4.7.5 exits 1 and `7z` 26.04 exits 2 (receipt `v2ns-readers`). Fact worth recording: the v2ns fixture differs from the standard v2 fixture **only inside the 4-byte magic word**, in two of its four bytes. `cmp -l` reports 2 differing bytes in each pair (receipt `v2ns-cmp`): 1-based positions 3 and 4 for the BE pair (`sqsh` → `sqlz`), 1 and 2 for the LE pair (`hsqs` → `zlqs`). So v2ns is a magic-spelling variant of a body whose extraction this round does measure; it is not promoted because its own magic has no second reader, not because its contents are unknown.
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:103:- `squashfs_v3_broadcom` (`shsq`/`qshs`): `unsquashfs` 4.7.5 refuses the magic (exit 1, "Can't find a valid SQUASHFS superblock"); `7z` 26.04 **does** list the committed LE broadcom fixture (exit 0, 2 files, 13 B; receipt `v3-readers`). A partial reader therefore exists, but no REA `extract-firmware` outcome was measured for this family and the C1 predicate is bound to v1/v2 carriers only; it stays Frontier.
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:106:- The promoted v1/v2 row covers gzip bodies only, because every v1/v2 carrier measured here is gzip. Nothing in this round measures a non-gzip v1/v2 body, so the row says nothing about one. [INFERENCE] whether a v1/v2 body with another compressor exists in the wild is not established by this entry.
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:115:**Unknown**: what bit `0x40` of the v1/v2 `flags` byte means; whether any SquashFS v1/v2 image outside these four extracts through REA at all; whether sasquatch's patches (S111) change any v1/v2 result relative to `unsquashfs` 4.7.5 beyond these four carriers; and which process wrote the committed receipts (R40-CL2).
29883d4ec35a31a8393c4ffb220888c44c0bb022:corpus/knowledge/2026-10-28-r49-squashfs-v1v2-core.md:119:Four 4 KiB fixtures carrying 3–4 regular files each are a thin carrier set: the entry says the handler family works through REA on these bytes, not that every v1/v2 image extracts. No symlink, no device node, no hard link, no sparse file and no multi-block member occurs in the set, so none of those paths is exercised. The second reader is `unsquashfs` from squashfs-tools 4.7.5, and the first reader is a patch set over the same codebase (see the oracle section); the entry claims a second reader from another release, not a second implementation. The unblob `__output__` tree is same-lineage and is used only as the producer's own expectation. The counter's rerun shows the carriers extract the same way at scoring time; it cannot prove which process wrote a committed receipt (R40-CL2). Nothing is claimed about the contents of the extracted text files beyond their sha256 and size.
